module Migrate exposing (Migrate)

{-| Reading a value that an earlier build of the program made, across an
upgrade that keeps a process's state (geng-lang `m2-beam-toptier.md` D458).

When a running BEAM program is upgraded to a new build, a server's state is
a value the old build's code made. The new build reads it with `migrate`, at
the type the new build declares for it:

    spec : Migrate state => { init : Init state, handleCall : ... } -> Spec state

The check is `Inbound`'s, made over Geng's own representation rather than the
shape a value crosses to Erlang in: a record keeps the fields its type
declares and drops the rest, a custom type is one of its constructors at the
constructor's arity, an `Array` is checked element by element, and a `Dict`
or a `Set`, which Geng holds as a tree, is read as one and built again under
the key's order in the new build. **A function or a `Task` is kept only when
the upgrade reloads none of its code**: a reloaded module's old code is
removed once the upgrade is done, and a function it made fails when it is
called then, so the check fails where it meets one. A value
the type cannot hold is a crash naming the first argument, the value, and
what the type admits.

Every type has an instance without asking, as it has [`Inbound`](Inbound).
On JavaScript nothing is upgraded in place, and the value is the term.

@docs Migrate

-}

import Extern exposing (Handle)
import String exposing (String)


{-| Types a value of an earlier build can be read as. `migrate where value`
is the value as one of the type, or a crash naming `where`.
-}
class Migrate a where
    migrate : String -> Handle -> a


{-| The check at the type of each use (D458). The compiler supplies every
instance as a reference to this row at the instance's type, and the BEAM
backend generates the check for that type in its place, so its `erlang` row
is never called.
-}
@externPure(js, "Migrate", "check")
@externPure(erlang, "geng_core_migrate", "check")
check : String -> Handle -> a
